%\SweaveUTF8

<<setRopts, echo=FALSE>>=
options(width = 80, prompt = "> ", continue = " ")
options(SweaveHooks = list(fig = function() par(mar = c(4, 4, 1.4, 0.7))))
@
\SweaveOpts{engine = R, keep.source = TRUE, strip.white = true}
\SweaveOpts{eps = FALSE, pdf = TRUE, width = 9, height = 6}
\setkeys{Gin}{width=\textwidth}

\begin{Problem}[Domain estimation]\label{problem:domain}
    We use the data set \code{sepe.dat} from Problem Set 1. Treat the data
    as a simple random sample without replacement (\SRS) of $n = 800$
    establishments from a population of size $N = 3770$. We focus on industry
    class 31, that is, the domain $U_{31}$ identified by \code{code == 1}.

\begin{Subtask}
    %---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    \item Assume that the domain size $N_{31}$ is unknown. Estimate the domain
        population mean $\bar{y}_{U31}$ of environmental-protection
        expenditures and its standard error.

        \begin{Rhint}
            If \code{dn} is a survey design created with \code{svydesign()},
            use \code{subset(dn, code == 1)} to restrict the design to the
            domain. Do not create a new \SRS design from the domain
            observations: under the original \SRS, the domain sample size
            $n_{31}$ is random.
        \end{Rhint}

    \begin{Solution}
        First load the data set \code{sepe.dat}, which is an \SRS of size
        $n=800$, and define the population size $N=3770$:

<<echo = FALSE>>=
load("sepe.RData")
sepe$I31 <- as.integer(sepe$code == 1)
N <- 3770
n <- 800
@

<<eval = FALSE>>=
sepe <- read.table("https://stat.ethz.ch/Teaching/Datasets/cas-das/sepe.dat",
                    header = TRUE)


sepe$I31 <- as.integer(sepe$code == 1)
N <- 3770
@

        \textbf{Calculation with the \code{survey} package:} First define the
        survey design object for the full sample:

<<>>=
library("survey")
sepe$fpc <- N
dn_sepe <- svydesign(ids = ~1, fpc = ~fpc, data = sepe)
@

        For domain estimation, it is important to distinguish between
        subsetting a survey design object and creating a new design object from
        the observations in the domain. The command

<<>>=
dn_sepe_31 <- subset(dn_sepe, I31 == 1)
@

        retains the original sampling design. Thus, establishments in industry
        class 31 are treated as a domain of the original \SRS of $n=800$
        establishments. In particular, the number $n_{31}$ of sampled domain
        units is random.

        The domain population mean $\bar{y}_{U31}$ and its standard error are
        estimated by

<<>>=
m_y_31 <- svymean(~expend, dn_sepe_31)
m_y_31
@

        By contrast, suppose that we first restrict the data to industry class
        31 and then define a new design:

<<>>=
sepe_31 <- subset(sepe, I31 == 1)
dn_sepe_31_new <- svydesign(ids = ~1, data = sepe_31)

svymean(~expend, dn_sepe_31_new)
@

        This specifies a different sampling design. It treats the $n_{31}$
        observed establishments as if they formed a new equal-probability
        sample, rather than a domain of the original \SRS. The estimated mean is
        the same because both estimators reduce to the sample mean within the
        domain. The estimated standard errors differ, however, because the two
        design objects represent different sampling designs.

        \begin{Remark}
            Let $\indicator_{i,31} = \indicator\{i\in U_{31}\}$ denote the
            domain indicator and let $s_{31} = s\cap U_{31}$. Then
            \begin{equation*}
                n_{31} = \sum_{i\in s}\indicator_{i,31}.
            \end{equation*}
            The domain mean is estimated by
            \begin{equation*}
                \bar{y}_{31} = \frac{\sum_{i\in s}\indicator_{i,31}\,y_i}{
                    \sum_{i\in s}\indicator_{i,31}}
                = \frac{1}{n_{31}}\sum_{i \in s_{31}}y_i.
            \end{equation*}
            Because $n_{31}$ is random under the original \SRS, this estimator
            can be viewed as a ratio estimator. A linearization variance
            estimator under \SRS is
            \begin{equation*}
                \widehat{\var}\left(\bar{y}_{31}\right) =
                (1-f) \frac{n(n_{31} - 1)}{n_{31}^2(n - 1)} \,
                    \widehat{\sigma}_{y,31}^2, \qquad f = \frac{n}{N},
            \end{equation*}
            where $\widehat{\sigma}_{y,31}^2$ is the sample variance of $y$
            among the $n_{31}$ sampled domain units.

            \textbf{Calculation with R:}

<<>>=
n31 <- sum(sepe$I31)
mean_y_31 <- mean(sepe$expend[sepe$I31 == 1])
sigma2_y_31 <- var(sepe$expend[sepe$I31 == 1])
se_mean_y_31 <- sqrt((1 - n / N) * n * (n31 - 1) /
                     (n31^2 * (n - 1)) * sigma2_y_31)
@

            The manually calculated quantities agree with those obtained from
            the subsetted survey design.

<<>>=
identical(as.numeric(coef(m_y_31)), mean_y_31)
all.equal(as.numeric(SE(m_y_31)), se_mean_y_31)
@
        \end{Remark}
\end{Solution}

    %---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    \item Now assume that the domain size $N_{31} = 650$ is known, but that
        this information was not used when the sample was drawn. Estimate the
        population domain total of environmental expenditures $t_{y,31}$ and
        its standard error.

    \begin{Solution}
        The sampling design remains an \SRS from the full population. Hence,
        $n_{31}$ remains random. Following the classical definition of the
        hypergeometric distribution,\footnote{See e.g., Johnson, N. L, Kemp, A.
            W., and Kotz, S. (2005). Univariate Discrete Distributions,
            3$^{\mathrm{rd}}$ ed., Hoboken (NJ): John Wiley \& Sons, p. 251.}
        consider a population of $N$ units, of which $N_{31}$ belong to domain
        $U_{31}$ and $N-N_{31}$ do not. If an \SRS of $n$ units is drawn without
        replacement, then the number $n_{31}$ of sampled domain units follows a
        hypergeometric distribution:
        \begin{equation}\label{eq:hyper}
            n_{31} \sim \operatorname{Hypergeometric}(N, N_{31}, n).
        \end{equation}

        The known domain size $N_{31}=650$ is used only at the estimation
        stage. The domain total is estimated by
        \begin{equation*}
            \widehat{t}_{y,31} = N_{31} \cdot \bar{y}_{31},
        \end{equation*}
        with estimated standard error
        \begin{equation*}
            \widehat{\se}(\,\widehat{t}_{y,31}) = N_{31} \cdot
                \widehat{\se}(\bar{y}_{31}).
        \end{equation*}
        The variance estimator for $\bar{y}_{31}$, and hence
        $\widehat{\se}(\bar{y}_{31})$, is the linearization variance estimator
        under \SRS introduced in part a).

        \textbf{Calculation with the \code{survey} package:} We use the
        subsetted survey design \code{dn_sepe_31} from part a). The estimated
        domain mean $\bar{y}_{31}$ has already been calculated; it is
        reproduced here for ease of reference:

<<>>=
m_y_31 <- svymean(~expend, dn_sepe_31)
@

        The estimated domain total and its standard error are then

<<>>=
N31 <- 650
c(total = N31 * coef(m_y_31), SE = N31 * SE(m_y_31))
@

        \begin{Remark}
            If the domain size $N_{31}$ is known, then $n_{31}$ has a
            hypergeometric distribution; see (\ref{eq:hyper}). In our
            case, $n_{31}$ has expected value $\approx 137.9$ and
            standard deviation $\approx 9.6$.
            Thus, the observed value $n_{31}=138$ is close to its expected
            value under the sampling design. For example, the central 80\%
            range of the distribution can be obtained with:

<<>>=
qhyper(c(0.1, 0.9), N31, N - N31, n)
@

            Note that R uses an equivalent but different parametrization from
            that in (\ref{eq:hyper}).

            Another remark is in order. The known domain size $N_{31}$ can also
            be incorporated by calibration. We can define the domain indicator
            as an auxiliary variable and calibrate the full design to the known
            totals $N$ and $N_{31}$:

<<>>=
dn_sepe_cal <- calibrate(dn_sepe, ~I31,
                          population = c(`(Intercept)` = N, I31 = N31))
svytotal(~expend, subset(dn_sepe_cal, I31 == 1))
@

        We will study calibration in more detail later.
        \end{Remark}
    \end{Solution}
\end{Subtask}
\end{Problem}
